.\" claudii manual — auto-synced to wiki via GitHub Actions
.TH CLAUDII 1 "June 15, 2026" "claudii 0.26.0" "claudii manual"
.
.SH NAME
claudii \- Fast Claude Code aliases with live model status and session insights
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claudii
.IR command " [" args ]
.PP
When invoked with no arguments,
.B claudii
shows a contextual summary: active session data, a rate-limit warning
(when 5h usage >= 80%), or a welcome message when no cache exists.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Quick start — enable everything at once:
.B claudii on
.TP
Live health check:
.B claudii status
.TP
Show active sessions with token throughput and rate limits:
.B claudii se
.TP
Response-time and throughput for one repo:
.B claudii perf --repo claudii
.TP
Read a config value:
.B claudii config get aliases.cl.model
.TP
Switch CC-Statusline to dense layout:
.B claudii cc-statusline preset focused
.TP
Toggle only ClaudeStatus RPROMPT:
.B claudii claudestatus off
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claudii
is a zsh plugin and CLI for Claude Code power users:
fast aliases to launch Claude with the right model and effort,
live model health in your RPROMPT, and a metrics statusline inside Claude Code sessions.
.
.SH INSTALL
.B Homebrew (recommended):
.PP
.RS
.nf
brew tap bmmmm/tap
brew install claudii
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add to
.IR ~/.zshrc :
.RS
.nf
source "$(brew --prefix)/opt/claudii/libexec/claudii.plugin.zsh"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Manual:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git clone https://github.com/bmmmm/claudii ~/claudii
bash ~/claudii/install.sh
.fi
.RE
.
.SH LAUNCHING CLAUDE
Shell functions for fast model access.
Configured under the
.B aliases.*
config namespace.
Auto-fallback to a healthy model when the configured model is down
.RB ( fallback.enabled ).
.TP
.B cl
Claude Sonnet, xhigh effort. General-purpose default.
.TP
.B clo
Claude Opus, xhigh effort. Complex tasks, server work.
.TP
.B clm
Claude Opus, xhigh effort. Hardest problems, maximum reasoning.
.TP
.B clq
Claude Sonnet, medium effort.
Launches in
.I search.dir
(default:
.IR ~/claude-search ).
Equivalent to
.BR "claudii search" .
.TP
.B clf
Claude Fable, xhigh effort. Anthropic's most capable model, for the hardest
reasoning and long-horizon agentic work.
.TP
.B clre
Resume a previous session.
Without an argument it opens the interactive session picker;
with a session id it resumes that session directly
.RB ( "clre " \fIid\fR ).
Passes no
.B \-\-model
or
.BR \-\-effort ,
so the session keeps the model it was started with;
extra flags are forwarded to
.BR claude .
.TP
.B clh
Show the alias table and run a live model health check.
.TP
.B clorch / cle
Example agent aliases — see
.BR "claudii agents" .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output structured JSON instead of formatted text.
Available for:
.BR status ", " sessions ", " cost ", " skills-cost ", " trends ", " doctor ", " version ", " agents ", " tokens ", " perf ", " tools ", " repos ", " limits ", " cache .
.TP
.B \-\-tsv
Output tab-separated values (header + data rows).
Available for:
.BR sessions ", " cost .
.PP
When stdout is not a terminal, ANSI colors are stripped and plain text is emitted.
.B version
prints only the bare version number when piped.
.
.SH DISPLAY LAYERS
Three separate layers each serve a different scope:
.TP
.B ClaudeStatus (RPROMPT)
Model health in the right-hand shell prompt. When every tracked model is
healthy it collapses to a single
.B claude \(dg
\(em an unnamed model is assumed working. Only down or degraded models are
named (Fable included); a plane-wide outage collapses to
.BR "claude \(da" .
Reads a local cache — no network calls in
.IR precmd .
Indicators:
.B \(dg
= ok,
.B \(ti
= degraded,
.B \(da
= down,
.B ?
= unreachable,
.B \(lB\(el\(rB
= loading,
.B \(rn
= refreshing.
.br
A trailing incident glyph mirrors status.claude.com:
.B \[u203C]
investigating,
.B \[u2690]
identified,
.B \[u25CE]
monitoring \(em stage-colored when a tracked model is actually affected.
When an incident exists but none of your tracked models are affected (e.g. a
note about models you don't use), it shows a neutral
.B \[u24D8]
instead of an alarm.
.TP
.B Session Dashboard (above prompt)
Per-session summary lines above the prompt, rendered via the precmd hook.
Shows model, context %, token throughput, 5h rate limit + countdown.
Only shown after
.B claudii
commands; suppressed after
.BR se / si / sessions .
.TP
.B CC-Statusline (inside Claude Code)
Metrics line inside Claude Code sessions via the
.B statusLine
hook in
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json .
Receives live JSON from Claude Code on each turn.
Segments configured via
.B statusline.lines
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.PP
Data flow:
.RS
.nf
Claude Code \(-> CC-Statusline (stdin JSON) \(-> cache \(-> Session Dashboard
                                            \(-> history-YYYY-MM.tsv
.fi
.RE
.
.SH COMMANDS
.
.SS Overview (bare claudii)
Running
.B claudii
with no arguments prints the Overview: a modular status page built from
sections. Default order: account (rate limits with reset countdown and
wall-clock reset time + today's token throughput), usage
(30-day token-per-day sparkline from history, with today/average/peak),
sessions (active/inactive/stale counts, next cron wake, bg tasks), activity
(mini vibemap strip), agents (configured agents grouped by model tier,
plus shell aliases), services (ClaudeStatus / Dashboard / CC-Statusline
state), commands (grouped quick reference).
.PP
Section order and selection are config-driven via
.B overview.sections
(array of section names) \(em remove a name to hide that section,
reorder the array to reorder the output. Unknown names render an
inline warning instead of failing.
.PP
Dim lines starting with \(->\& list invocable commands only \(em
never data.
.
.SS Display Layer Control
.TP
.B on
Enable all three layers at once (ClaudeStatus, Session Dashboard, CC-Statusline).
When
.B presence.file
is configured, removes the presence file (re-enables Claude Code mobile notifications).
.TP
.B off
Disable all three layers at once.
When
.B presence.file
is configured, touches the presence file (suppresses Claude Code mobile notifications via
.BR CLAUDE_CLIENT_PRESENCE_FILE ) .
.TP
.BR "claudestatus " [on|off]
Toggle only the ClaudeStatus RPROMPT.
Without argument: show current state.
.TP
.BR "session-dashboard " [on|off]
Toggle only the Session Dashboard above the prompt.
Without argument: show current state.
Deprecated alias:
.BR dashboard .
.TP
.BR "cc-statusline " [on|off]
Toggle only the CC-Statusline inside Claude Code.
Modifies
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json .
Without argument: show current state.
.TP
.BI "cc-statusline preset " [name]
Switch the CC-Statusline layout to a named preset.
Without a name, lists available presets.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B focused
\(em dense 3-line layout: identity / metrics / env.
.IP \(bu 2
.B calm
\(em bare 2-line layout: model / context-bar.
.IP \(bu 2
.B default
\(em restore the shipped 5-line layout.
.RE
.IP
After switching, restart Claude Code to see the change.
Writes
.B statusline.lines
to
.IR config.json .
.TP
.BR "insomnii " [on|off|auto|status|install]
Manage the optional
.BR cc-insomnii (1)
bedtime clock segment.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B on
\(em require delegation; doctor warns if the binary is missing.
.IP \(bu 2
.B off
\(em suppress delegation; the clock segment is empty.
.IP \(bu 2
.B auto
\(em delegate when installed, skip when not (default).
.IP \(bu 2
.B status
(or no argument) \(em show binary path and active mode.
.IP \(bu 2
.B install
\(em clone
.B $CC_INSOMNII_REPO
(default
.IR https://github.com/bmmmm/cc-insomnii )
and run its
.BR install.sh .
Idempotent.
.RE
.
.SS Model Health (ClaudeStatus)
.TP
.B status
Live model health check (bypasses cache).
Per-model status table with icons, last-check time, active incidents, and
recent state transitions (from the status history log).
Absolute timestamps honor
.BR display.timezone .
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
Alias:
.BR s .
.TP
.BR "status " 5m|15m|30m|60m
Set the base background cache refresh interval (default: 15m).
Values below 30s are rejected.
The effective interval adapts to the last known state: 2\(mu base while
healthy, base\(di5 (min 60s) during incidents, base when the status API is
unreachable.
.TP
.BR "status \-\-history " [\-\-days\ N]
Print the full model-state transition log from
.IR status-history.tsv ,
newest first (bare
.B status
shows only the last 5).
.B \-\-days\ N
limits output to transitions within the last
.I N
days.
Absolute timestamps honor
.BR display.timezone .
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
.
.SS Sessions and Cost
.TP
.B sessions
List active and recent sessions: model, context %, token throughput with
cache-hit rate, rate limits, worktree, agent context, and a fingerprint of
top-5 files accessed. Cost is no longer in this view \(em see
.BR cost .
Background sessions
.RB ( "claude --bg" )
are marked
.BR [bg] .
Supports
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-tsv .
Aliases:
.BR se ", " ss .
.TP
.BI "sessions \-\-resume " id
Resume a session by ID.
Equivalent to
.BR "claude --resume " id .
.TP
.B sessions-inactive
Inactive (ended or stale) sessions with age, context, and rate-limit data.
Shows badges: pinned, stale, or idle.
Alias:
.BR si .
.TP
.BI "session " session-id
Per-session drilldown from the insights cache, matched by session-id substring
(first match wins). Shows the token split (input / output / cache write as share
bars, plus cache read with its hit rate and the thinking-block count), the
top tools with per-tool error markers, and compact lines for subagents, stop
reasons, and rate-limit hits (account-wide). Live context %, model, and project
come from the
.I session-*
cache when one still exists; for ended sessions the model is inferred from the
token data and the context bar is omitted.
.TP
.BI "pin " session-id
Protect an inactive session from garbage collection.
Matches by session ID substring (first match wins).
.TP
.BI "unpin " session-id
Remove GC protection from a pinned session.
.TP
.B gc
Remove stale session cache files.
Pinned sessions are never removed.
Run
.B claudii si
to inspect before garbage collecting.
With
.BI "\-\-insights " DAYS
additionally prunes orphaned insights caches (sessions whose source transcript
Claude Code already deleted) older than
.I DAYS
days.
Dry-run by default \(em add
.B \-\-yes
to delete.
Orphaned caches are the only cost history beyond Claude Code's transcript
retention; caches whose transcript still exists are never touched.
Alias:
.BR g .
.TP
.BI "resume " session-id
Resume a session by ID
.RB ( "claude --resume " id ).
Use
.B claudii se
to list IDs.
.TP
.B cost
The dollar view (token throughput lives in the other commands). Per-period spend
with a per-tier breakdown: Today and Week show share bars sorted by amount plus
the period token total; Months and Years render as a column matrix (model tiers
\(mu periods) with row and column totals. Reads from
.I ~/.cache/claudii/history-YYYY-MM.tsv
(written by the CC-Statusline handler).
Supports
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-tsv .
Alias:
.BR c .
.RS
.PP
With
.B \-\-window
the Week section is replaced by
.BR Window :
the same per-tier breakdown, but over Anthropic rolling 7-day rate-limit
window instead of the calendar week set by
.IR cost.week_start .
The window boundary sits mid-day, so this bucket is cut on row timestamps
rather than on dates. Prints a notice and exits when no window is known yet.
See
.BR week .
.PP
With
.B \-\-forecast
the spend tables are replaced by a burn-rate projection in two blocks. The
.B 5h budget
block shows the current account-wide rate-limit usage, the burn rate (the slope
of
.I rate_5h
over the last 20 minutes, with an accelerating/slowing note), a projected
time-to-limit when the budget is on track to be hit before the window resets,
and the reset countdown \(em the live state comes from the newest active
.I session-*
cache (history carries no reset timestamp), so it degrades to a month-only view
when no session is live. The
.B "This month"
block shows spend so far, the $/day pace, the month-end projection with an
honest \(+- band derived from daily-spend variance, and a comparison against
last month. Honors
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-tsv .
.RE
.TP
.B week
Usage inside Anthropic rolling 7-day rate-limit window \(em the quota that
actually gates work, as opposed to the calendar week
.B cost
reports. Shows tokens, dollars and session count consumed since the window
opened, the used percentage, the reset countdown, and the daily pace with the
moment the quota runs dry if that falls before the reset.
.RS
.PP
The window itself comes from
.I rate_limits.seven_day
in the CC-Statusline payload; its start is the reset epoch minus seven days.
Claude Code reports the window only after a session's first API response and
drops it once the reset passes, so an empty window is a normal state, not an
error.
.PP
The
.B quota
line carries a token estimate Anthropic does not publish. It is labelled
.I estimated
while it is derived from tokens-so-far over percent-used, and
.I measured
once a wide enough percentage spread inside one window allows deriving it from
\(*Dtokens instead. Both remain approximations \(em Anthropic weighs models and
cache differently than raw token counts. A Max plan also carries a second,
Opus-only weekly limit that the payload does not expose; use
.B /usage
in Claude Code to see both.
.PP
.B \-\-history " [" N ]
draws one bar per window over the last
.I N
windows (default 8), so a week reads against its neighbours rather than in
isolation.
.PP
Anthropic sometimes ends a weekly window early for technical reasons; the reset
it had announced then never fires. Because of that, claudii derives a window
boundary from the point where the reported reset
.I changes
\(em recorded per row \(em and only falls back to a 7-day grid where nothing was
recorded. Reconstructed boundaries are marked
.RB ( ~ )
and a window that came in short is labelled
.BR short .
Windows predating this feature are all reconstructed, since Claude Code reports
no past resets.
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
Alias:
.BR w .
.RE
.TP
.B skills-cost
Per-skill (or per-plugin, per-MCP-tool) cost breakdown using attribution data
from
.BR claudii-insights .
Shows calls, total cost, average cost per call, the dominant model
(the model behind \[>=]80% of a row's attributed calls, otherwise
.BR mixed ),
and an outlier flag
.RB ( ! )
when a row's average is \[>=]2\[mu] the median with at least 10 calls.
Subagent usage counts toward the skill that spawned the agent.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Rolling window (default 30). Passed to
.BR "claudii-insights merge" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-compare " BEFORE : AFTER
Trend comparison of two windows: the prior period
.RI "[now\-(" BEFORE + AFTER "), now\-" AFTER ")"
against the recent
.RI "[now\-" AFTER ", now]"
\(em e.g.
.B "\-\-compare 30:30"
is the previous 30 days vs the last 30.
The headline metric is
.BR out-tokens/call ,
which (unlike $/call) does not scale with session context size, so it isolates
whether a skill talks less after a
.I SKILL.md
edit; $/call is shown but flagged context-confounded.
Honors
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-plugins
Show
.B attribution_plugins
instead of
.B attribution_skills
(same column layout).
.TP
.B \-\-mcp
Show
.B attribution_mcp
\(em per-MCP-tool costs. A message's usage is split evenly across the MCP
tools it invokes, so totals stay comparable with the skills table.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit rows + outlier flag as JSON to stdout (parseable, no color codes).
Each row carries the token split
.RB ( in_tok ", " out_tok ", " cache_read ", " cache_create )
\(em the signal that separates \(lqskill talks too much\(rq (out-heavy)
from \(lqskill runs in fat sessions\(rq (cache_read-heavy).
Costs are priced per model: each per-model token bucket
.RB ( attribution_models ", "
schema v5) bills at its own tier rate (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, Fable; cache_read
0.1\(mu, cache_create 1.25\(mu input). Pre-v5 / orphaned caches carry no
per-model split \(em their residual tokens fall back to the flat Sonnet rate.
.B meta.pricing
documents the rates and the residual fallback.
.RE
.TP
.B trends
Token-primary rolling 7-day chart: daily token bars (normalized to the busiest
day) with spend as a dim side column, a 30-day model split by tokens, busiest
day, average API duration per session, and the 7d-vs-30d token trend.
Reads from
.I ~/.cache/claudii/history-YYYY-MM.tsv
(written by the CC-Statusline handler).
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
Alias:
.BR t .
.TP
.B tokens
Token breakdown for the rolling window (default: 7 days), aggregated from the
JSONL transcripts under
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
Input+output is the primary figure; cache read/write and the cache-hit rate
.RB ( cache_read " / (" cache_read " + " cache_create " + " input ))
are shown alongside. Three sections:
.B By type
(throughput split across cache read / input / cache write / output, as share
bars),
.B By model
(a per-model matrix of input / output / cache rd / cache wr / hit), and
.B By day
(in+output per calendar day, bars normalized to the busiest day, with the
day-level hit rate). All three are floored to exactly
.I N
calendar days so the window matches the header.
.RS
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
The word forms
.BR day ", " week ", " month ", " quarter
are also accepted (1 / 7 / 30 / 90 days).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (the curated aggregate, raw model ids) instead
of the formatted tables.
.RE
.TP
.B tools
Tool / efficiency lens over the rolling window (default: 7 days): a table of the
top tools by call count with share bars and per-tool error rate (a
.B \(rq
flag marks rates above 5%), the subagent types spawned, total thinking-block
volume, and a one-line callout of the highest-error-rate tool. The
throughput/reliability companion to
.B skills-cost
(which is cost-focused). MCP tool names are shortened to their tool segment and
the long tail is folded into a
.B +N more
line.
.RS
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
The word forms
.BR day ", " week ", " month ", " quarter
are also accepted (1 / 7 / 30 / 90 days).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (the curated aggregate, raw model ids) instead
of the formatted tables.
.RE
.TP
.B repos [REPO]
Sessions per repository for the rolling window (default: 30 days): how many
sessions ran in which repo and how long they really took. Duration is the
.B active
time \(em gap-capped main-thread activity where pauses longer than 10 minutes
count as idle \(em not the wall-clock span, which idle time and resumed
sessions inflate (shown alongside as
.BR span ).
The default view is a per-repo table (sessions, median active, total active,
total span). Headless noise \(em TMPDIR runs and micro sessions with under
2 minutes of activity \(em folds into one dim
.B (headless)
summary line. A positional
.I REPO
argument lists that repo's sessions one per row (day, active, span, messages,
session id).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-daily
Per-day breakdown instead of the repo table: one row per calendar day
(newest first) listing each active repo with its session count and active time.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include headless/micro sessions in the repo and daily views (TMPDIR sessions
appear as
.BR (tmp) ).
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
The word forms
.BR day ", " week ", " month ", " quarter
are also accepted (1 / 7 / 30 / 90 days).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (by_repo, by_day, sessions, headless) instead
of the formatted tables.
.RE
.TP
.B limits
Rate-limit hits in the rolling window (default: 7 days) from the insights
.IR limit_hits .
Groups the hits by day (newest-first, a bar scaled to the busiest day plus that
day's hit count), an hour strip showing when hits cluster, and a per-model
tally. Time and model only
\(em the data carries no percentage, and the 5h budget is account-wide, so the
model is \(lqwhat ran\(rq, not \(lqwhat is to blame\(rq. With no hits it reports
clear runway.
.RS
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
The word forms
.BR day ", " week ", " month ", " quarter
are also accepted (1 / 7 / 30 / 90 days).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (the curated aggregate, raw model ids) instead
of the formatted tables.
.RE
.TP
.B cache
Prompt-cache hit rate per day and per model (default: 7-day window).
Aggregated from JSONL transcripts under
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
First run is slower (5\(en30 s) while the cache warms; subsequent runs
only re-aggregate changed sessions.
.RS
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
The word forms
.BR day ", " week ", " month ", " quarter
are also accepted (1 / 7 / 30 / 90 days).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (the curated aggregate, raw model ids) instead
of the formatted tables.
.RE
.TP
.B perf
Response-time and throughput dashboard for the rolling window (default: 7 days).
Latency is estimated from the JSONL transcripts as the wall-clock gap between an
assistant response and its parent record
.RB ( assistant.timestamp " \(mi " parent.timestamp ),
main-thread responses only (sidechains interleave concurrently and are excluded).
Sections:
.B By model
(response-time
.BR p50 / p90 / p99
and output
.BR tok/s ),
.B By context window
(latency bucketed by context-window occupancy \(em
.IR input " + " cache_read " + " cache_creation
tokens \(em so the response-time cost of a large window is visible:
.BR p50 / p90 / p99
climb and the
.B p99
tail explodes as the window grows),
a
.B Latency trend
(per-day p50 sparkline with a today-vs-baseline delta as an early-warning
signal), a
.B By repo
breakdown, and a one-line
.B API health
read from the ClaudeStatus cache. Throughput is output tokens over end-to-end
time (includes wait).
.PP
With the optional OpenTelemetry source enabled
.RB ( "claudii\-otel setup" ,
which flips the config flag, writes the shell env the plugin sources, and
generates a launchd plist;
.B claudii\-otel off
reverts), perf reads exact metrics instead of the transcript estimate: real
.I duration_ms
and
.I ttft_ms
(time to first token, the
.B TTFT
line), the per-response
.B success
rate with retry count, and an
.B "API errors"
breakdown by HTTP status code from
.I api_error
events. The local receiver
.RB ( "claudii\-otel receiver" )
captures Claude Code's OTLP/JSON export under
.IR ~/.cache/claudii/otel/ ;
by default nothing leaves the machine. Setting
.I perf.otel.forward
to an internal collector URL makes the receiver additionally mirror each batch
there (best-effort, for central retention on a shared gateway); claudii itself
still reads only the local file. The active source is shown in the header
.RB ( "source: otel" " vs " "source: transcript" ).
.B claudii\-otel doctor
reports whether the receiver, launchd job, and config flag agree and how fresh
the captured data is \(em the first stop when
.B perf
still shows
.BR "source: transcript" .
.RS
.TP
.B today | 7d | 30d | 90d | year
Cycle the rolling window without
.BR \-\-days
\(em a named window or any
.IR N d
token (e.g.
.IR 14d ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-days " N
Override the rolling window with an explicit day count.
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo " NAME
Narrow every section to one repository; the
.B By repo
breakdown is replaced by a
.B By session
breakdown within that repo.
.TP
.BI "\-\-watch" "[=N]"
Clear the screen and re-render every
.I N
seconds (default: 30). Press Ctrl-C to exit. Compatible with all other flags.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the view as structured JSON (percentiles in milliseconds, raw model ids)
instead of the formatted tables.
.RE
.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B config
Show all config values.
.TP
.BI "config get " key
Read a value using dot-notation.
Example:
.BR "claudii config get aliases.cl.model" .
.TP
.BI "config set " "key value"
Set a value.
Example:
.BR "claudii config set statusline.models opus,sonnet" .
.TP
.B "config reset"
Reset all config to defaults.
.TP
.BI "config export " "[file]"
Print config as JSON, or write to
.I file
if specified.
.TP
.BI "config import " file
Replace config with a JSON file (backs up existing config first).
.TP
.B "config theme"
List available color themes. Active theme marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.BI "config theme " name
Set the active theme.
Available:
.B default
(magenta accent, standard ANSI) and
.B pastel
(softer 256-color palette).
Stored as
.B theme.name
in
.IR config.json .
.TP
.B agents
List configured agents as a table with columns
.IR ALIAS ", " SKILL ", " MODEL ", " EFFORT ", " DESCRIPTION .
With no agents configured, shows onboarding instructions instead.
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
Configure via:
.RS
.nf
claudii config set agents.clorch.skill orchestrate
claudii config set agents.clorch.model opus
claudii config set agents.clorch.effort high
claudii config set agents.clorch.description "Orchestrator"
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "search " "['query']"
Launch Claude Code in the configured search directory
.RI ( search.dir ,
default:
.IR ~/claude-search ).
If a
.I query
is provided it is passed as the initial prompt.
.
.SS Local LLM (omlx / gateii)
.TP
.B "omlx status"
Show whether the omlx segment is wired in, the
.I active.json
path, and oMLX server reachability.
.TP
.B "omlx connect"
Detect a gateii install, store the path, and probe the server.
.TP
.B "omlx test"
Render the cc-statusline against a synthetic
.I active.json
with the omlx segment.
.TP
.B "omlx disconnect"
Remove the
.B omlx
entry from any custom
.B statusline.lines
layout.
.PP
Override the
.I active.json
path via
.B CLAUDII_OMLX_ACTIVE
or config key
.BR statusline.omlx_active_path .
.
.SS VPN State (vpnii)
Shows the active WireGuard tunnel name in the cc-statusline and RPROMPT.
State file:
.IR ~/.cache/claudii/vpnii .
.B claudii vpnii
wraps write/clear with privilege drop so the file is always user-owned.
.PP
Recommended wg-quick config:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[Interface]
\&...
PostUp  = claudii vpnii set HomeLab
PreDown = claudii vpnii clear
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI "vpnii set " "<name>"
Mark tunnel
.I name
as active.
Resolves the target user via
.B SUDO_USER
\(rA console owner \(rA
.B $USER
\(rA
.BR CLAUDII_USER .
.TP
.B "vpnii clear"
Remove the state file.
Aliases:
.BR "vpnii down" ", " "vpnii off" .
.TP
.B "vpnii show"
Print the current tunnel name, or
.I "no tunnel"
when none is set.
Alias:
.BR "vpnii status" .
.PP
Tailscale is detected separately via interface scan
(IPv4 in 100.64.0.0/10) and needs no state file \(em it renders as its own
.B tailscale
statusline segment, independent of
.BR vpn .
.
.SS Activity Heatmap (vibemap)
Opt-in prompt-timing tracker, rendered as an ASCII heatmap.
Default
.BR off .
When enabled, the cc-statusline appends one TSV line per render to
.IR ~/.cache/claudii/vibemap.tsv .
Schema:
.BR "epoch \\\\t weekday \\\\t hour \\\\t minute \\\\t model \\\\t sid8 \\\\t delta_ms" .
No prompt content is recorded.
.TP
.B vibemap
Last 30 days \[mu] 24-hour strip (default view).
Today\[aq]s row is highlighted with a \[u25B6] marker and a cursor at the current hour.
.TP
.BI "vibemap strip" " [--days N]"
The same strip with a custom window: last
.I N
days \[mu] 24 hours (default 30, range 1\(en90).
.TP
.B "vibemap grid"
Weekday \[mu] 3-hour-bin grid.
Today\[aq]s weekday column is highlighted in accent color with a \[u25B6] prefix.
.TP
.B "vibemap status"
Show enabled state, file path, entry count, and oldest entry.
.TP
.B "vibemap path"
Print the vibemap file path.
.TP
.B "vibemap clear"
Remove the vibemap file.
.PP
Enable:
.BR "claudii config set vibemap.enabled true" .
.
.SS System
.TP
.B explain
Component overview with current status, data flow, and symbol legend.
Aliases:
.BR layers ", " components .
.TP
.B restart
Reload the plugin by sourcing
.IR ~/.zshrc .
.TP
.B update
Update claudii.
Auto-detects Homebrew (runs
.BR "brew upgrade claudii" )
or git
.RB ( "git pull --ff-only" ).
Run
.B claudii restart
after updating.
.TP
.B version
Print version. When piped, prints the bare number only.
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B changelog
Release notes for the current version.
Alias:
.BR about .
.TP
.B doctor
Installation health check: Claude Code, jq, CC-Statusline config, cache directory,
completions, plugin load, cc-insomnii state, and version.
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
Alias:
.BR d .
.
.SH SHORTCUTS
.PP
.TS
l l.
s	status
se	sessions
ss	sessions
si	sessions-inactive
c	cost
t	trends
d	doctor
g	gc
about	changelog
.TE
.
.SH CC-STATUSLINE SEGMENTS
Each segment is separated by a dim
.B \(bv
character and omitted when its data is absent.
The layout is controlled by
.B statusline.lines
(array of arrays; each inner array is one output line).
.PP
.TS
l l l.
Segment	Example	Description
_
model	\fBClaude Opus 4.8\fR	Model name (bold); effort level appended (ultracode/xhigh/max highlighted); \[u25B2] when thinking active
cc-version	v2.1.90 +2	Claude Code app/CLI version, from the statusLine payload's top-level \fIversion\fR field. Dim when current; red with a \fB+N\fR releases-behind count when a newer build exists. See below
context	\[u25D1] 62%	Context fill as one glyph + usable% (\[u25CB]\[u25D4]\[u25D1]\[u25D5]\[u25CF] ramp, threshold-coloured). The compact default
context-bar	\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2591]\[u2591]\[u2591]\[u2591]\[u2591] 62%	Same value as a 10-block bar \(em ten columns instead of one
compact-eta	\[u21F2]18m	Time until the compact point at the current context burn rate. See below
cache-hit	\[u26A1]73%	Prompt-cache hit ratio (green >= 60%, yellow < 30%). Native \fIprompt_cache.hit_ratio\fR on Claude Code 2.1.251+, derived from the context window on older builds
cache-ttl	\[u2668]4m	Prompt-cache TTL: countdown until the cached prefix goes cold (yellow <= 2 min); cold shows the re-cache price. See below. Requires Claude Code 2.1.251+
response	\[u21AF]4.1s	API time the last response took (dim < 15s, yellow >= 30s, red >= 60s). See below
rate-5h	5h:17% \[u21BA]249m	5h rate limit + reset countdown (Pro/Max plans only)
rate-7d	7d:50% \[u21BA]3d15h	7d rate limit + reset countdown
cost	$0.42	Session cost in USD (dim \(em demoted by the token-first shift); omitted when zero
tokens	42.1K\(ua 8.3K\(da	Input / output tokens (cyan \(em primary throughput signal); K/M/B suffixes
lines-changed	+823 \-201	Git lines added (green) / removed (red); session total
duration	1h 23m	Wall-clock session duration (dim)
api-duration	api:45m	Time waiting for Claude API responses (dim)
burn-eta	ETA:180m	Estimated time until 5h limit exhausted (dim; requires >= 3 min session)
pace	\[u2191] / = / \[u2193]	Burn-pace tri-state vs linear: \[u2191] ahead (green), = on-pace (dim), \[u2193] behind (yellow). Opt-in only.
cron	\[u23F0] 42m	Time until next scheduled cron run (from Stop-hook session_crons). Opt-in only.
bg-tasks	\[u2699] 2bg	Number of background tasks pending (from Stop-hook bg_tasks). Opt-in only.
compactions	\[u267B]2	Compactions this session survived (detected via context-usage collapse). Opt-in only.
delta-5h	\[u03B4]5h:+12%	5h rate change since session start
delta-7d	\[u03B4]7d:+3%	7d rate change since session start
cache-create	\[u270E]1.2K	Tokens written into prompt cache this session
session-name	my-feature	Custom session name (from /rename or --name)
worktree	feat/login \[u23B7] main	Worktree + branch (dim) \(em the one this session runs in
worktrees	\[u2442]3 \[u232B]1	Linked worktrees in this repo (cyan), prunable ones called out as \[u232B]P (yellow). See below
dir	\[u2302] my-app	Working directory basename (dim)
github	\[u25C6] bmmmm/claudii #42	GitHub repo + active PR number (Claude Code 2.1.145+)
remotes	\[u21E1]fj\[u00B7]gh	Where the repo is published (from git remotes) \(em \fBfj\fR self-hosted forge (any non-GitHub host) / \fBgh\fR GitHub / \fBlocal\fR no remote
git-sync	\[u25CF]2 \[u2191]1 \[u2193]1	Working-copy sync state vs.\& upstream. See the colour key below
branch	\[u2387] main	Current branch (dim; yellow when uncommitted work sits on main/master/trunk, or on a detached HEAD). See below
ci	CI \[u2713]	Newest GitHub Actions run on this branch. See below
sessions	\[u26A0]2	Opt-in (not in the default layout; add via \fBstatusline.lines\fR): yellow \[u26A0]N for N OTHER live Claude Code sessions working in the same project/repo \(em including sessions in subdirectories or \fB.claude/worktrees/*\fR of it \(em so a session notices a parallel one moving git state under it. Empty when alone. Reads the existing per-session cache files (no process polling); the file scan only runs when \fBsessions\fR is in the active layout.
agent	@orc	Agent name prefixed @ (dim)
ruler	\(em\(em\(em\(em	Horizontal rule filling remaining terminal width
vpn	\[u2B21] HomeLab	Active WireGuard tunnel name, from the \fBvpnii\fR state file. Empty when no tunnel is active
tailscale	\[u2B22] ts	Tailscale reachability (CGNAT 100.64.0.0/10 interface scan, cached ~30s). Its own segment \(em not glued to \fBvpn\fR \(em so the two can sit on different lines
claude-status	Opus \[u2713]  Sonnet \[u2713]  Haiku \[u2713]  Fable \[u2713]	Model health from ClaudeStatus cache (auto-refreshes when older than status.cache_ttl)
omlx	\[u26A1] digest qwen3-30b-a3b 42s	Active local-agent run from \fBgateii\fR's \fIactive.json\fR \(em task, model and age. Hidden once the run is older than 300s
proxy	\[u21C4] gateii	Where this session's API traffic goes, read from \fIANTHROPIC_BASE_URL\fR in the project's \fB.claude/settings.local.json\fR (\fBgateii\fR for localhost:8888, otherwise the host). Dim \[u2192] api when that file exists without the key; empty when it does not
bumpii	\[u2B06] 2 \[u2709] 8	Pending tool updates and unread release news, from the \fBbumpii\fR(1)-backed cache. Opt-in. See below
clock	\[u263E] 23:30 +30m	Local time + bedtime nudge; delegates to \fBcc-insomnii\fR(1) when installed
.TE
.PP
.B context / context-bar color thresholds:
green < 70%, yellow 70\(en89%, red >= 90% of the
.I usable
context \(em the raw used-percentage rescaled to the model's practical (pre-compact)
window, so the display fills as the compact point nears rather than at 100%.
Both segments render the same number; they differ only in width \(em
.B context
spends one column on a
.BR \[u25CB]\[u25D4]\[u25D1]\[u25D5]\[u25CF]
fill glyph (the level stays readable without colour), while
.B context-bar
spends ten on the block bar. The
practical window is model-aware: flat-1M-billing models \(em opus, fable,
mythos, and sonnet 5+ (incl.\& their 1M window) \(em end at 80%, while a
legacy-sonnet model (4.6 and earlier) whose window exceeds ~244k (e.g.\&
sonnet[1m]) ends at a 195k habit ceiling \(em so a 1M sonnet[1m] bar fills
near 195k, not 800k. Sonnet 5, Fable, and Mythos ship 1M as their default
window (no [1m] opt-in needed), so they get the same flat-rate treatment as
opus here.
.B CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW
(inherited from Claude Code) overrides the default scale.
A
.B >200k
marker is appended when the context exceeds 200k tokens on a model where that
still means something, and it is
.IR model-aware .
Opus, Fable, Mythos, and Sonnet 5+ bill a flat rate across their entire 1M
window (no >200k long-context premium; Opus's real capacity edge is the ~780k
compact point, not 200k), so Claude Code's
.I exceeds_200k_tokens
flag is a non-event there and no marker is shown at all. For a legacy-sonnet
model (4.6 and earlier), crossing 200k still matters: on a native 1M window
(e.g.\& sonnet[1m], a paid opt-in) it is the long-context pricing tier, shown
as a yellow
.BR >200k ;
on a 200k-class window it is a genuine overflow of the standard window, shown as
a red
.BR >200k .
No constant window label is rendered \(em a dim
.B 1M
next to every reading said nothing that ever changed. The prompt-cache hit
ratio moved out of this segment into its own opt-in
.BR cache-hit .
.PP
.B cc-version:
red instead of dim, with a
.B +N
suffix, once a newer Claude Code build exists.
.I N
is exact \(em the position of the installed version inside a cached list of
recent release tags, not a version-number diff \(em because Claude Code's
release numbers are not sequential (a tag gets skipped/yanked now and then),
so
.RI "\[u201C]" 2.1.233\ vs.\&\ 2.1.231 "\[u201D] "
is not reliably \[u201C]2 releases\[u201D]. A
.B +?
means the installed version fell out of the cached window \(em definitely
outdated, count unknown \(em rather than a fabricated number. No cache yet
(first run, or the background check never having succeeded) renders the
plain dim version, same as up to date, until there is an actual answer. No
render ever makes a network call:
.BR claudii-cc-update-refresh (1)
maintains
.I status-cc-version
in the background under the TTL
.RB ( cc_version.cache_ttl ,
default 3600s), asking GitHub's public releases API for
.IR anthropics/claude-code .
The cache schema (
.IR latest= / tags= / checked= )
is the only contract a future refresher needs to honor \(em a
.BR bumpii (1)-backed
one could populate the same file without this segment changing at all.
.PP
.B bumpii:
.B \[u2B06]N
tracked tools with a newer release, and
.B \[u2709]K
repos with unread release news \(em both yellow, both omitted at zero. A half
that could not be checked renders a dim
.B ?
instead of a zero:
.RI \[u201C] "nothing pending" \[u201D]
is the one answer a broken update check must never give. The same rule ages
the whole segment \(em past 26h the numbers are yesterday's, and it reads a
dim
.BR "\[u2B06] stale" .
No render makes a network call:
.BR claudii-bumpii-refresh (1)
maintains
.I status-bumpii
in the background under the TTL
.RB ( bumpii.cache_ttl ,
default 21600s), which tightens to TTL/12 (min 5m) while anything is showing,
so a machine you just upgraded clears within minutes rather than at the TTL
mark. Opt-in \(em add
.B bumpii
to
.BR statusline.lines .
.PP
.B git-sync color key:
the working copy's state vs.\& its upstream, as a compact traffic light \(em
.B \[u25CF]N
uncommitted paths (commit pending, yellow),
.B \[u2191]N
commits ahead (push pending, yellow),
.B \[u2193]N
commits behind (pull pending, red \(em you're missing commits, the highest
merge-conflict risk). Clean and in sync shows a green
.BR \[u2713] .
A single
.B git status
fork per render scans the working tree; it ships in the default layout, but the
layout gate keeps the fork off for any custom
.B statusline.lines
that omits
.BR git-sync .
No upstream or a detached HEAD shows only the dirty state;
outside a git repo the segment is empty.
.PP
.B WIDTH \(em keeping a line from wrapping:
three mechanisms, applied in this order, because a statusline that wraps is
worse than one that says less.
.B statusline.max_label
(default 20) caps the only unbounded values a line carries \(em branch,
directory, worktree, session and agent names \(em with a trailing
.BR … ;
everything else is a number or a fixed glyph. It also strips the
.B worktree-
prefix Claude Code puts on worktree branch names, since the segments next to it
already say it is a worktree. 0 disables it.
.B statusline.separator
picks
.B tight
(default,
.RB \(dq " │ " \(dq )
or
.B wide
(the historical
.RB \(dq "  │  " \(dq );
two columns per gap is invisible until a dense line has eight of them.
.B statusline.max_width
(default 0 = off) is the backstop: a hard per-line column budget. A segment
that does not fit is skipped and the line continues \(em one wide segment (a
long branch name) must not take every cheap segment behind it down with it,
since
.BR ⇡fj·gh ,
.B ●3
and
.B ⑂1
cost three columns each. A line that lost anything ends in a dim
.BR … ,
so a shortened line always shows that it was shortened. The first segment of a
line always renders in full \(em a line reduced to nothing but an ellipsis
carries no information \(em which is the one case where the budget can be
exceeded.
.PP
All three count characters, not bytes, in every locale: under
.B LC_ALL=C
bash's
.B ${#…}
counts bytes and every glyph here is a three-byte sequence, so a column budget
measured that way reports overflows that do not exist \(em and a cut made that
way can land inside a character. Lengths therefore drop UTF-8 continuation
bytes before measuring, and a byte-mode cut backs off to the nearest character
boundary, which only ever shortens the result.
.PP
.B max_width
is deliberately not auto-detected. Nothing in the statusLine JSON carries the
terminal width, and the usual fallbacks lie in exactly this context: Claude
Code hands the command a
.B COLUMNS
of 0, and
.B tput cols
with a piped stdout answers with terminfo's 80 no matter how wide the window
really is. Truncating a 140-column terminal to 80 on a guess is worse than not
truncating at all \(em so the number is yours to set.
.PP
.B cache-ttl:
the prompt cache as a decision, not a statistic. While the cached prefix is
warm the segment counts down to the moment it goes cold
.RI ( prompt_cache.expires_at ,
computed in the same jq pass \(em no extra fork), turning yellow at two
minutes. Once it is cold \(em and only when the session has actually seen
caching, so providers that never report cache tokens stay silent \(em it shows
what continuing costs:
.IR prompt_cache.recache_tokens_if_cold ,
the tokens the next request re-caches, e.g.
.BR "\[u2668]cold\[u00B7]312.0K" .
That is the number behind "if I pause now, what does resuming cost?".
Absent entirely on Claude Code builds before 2.1.251 and before the first API
response. The statistics themselves (misses, expected rebuilds) stay in
.BR "/cost" .
.PP
.B compact-eta:
how long the remaining context lasts at the rate you are actually filling it \(em
the question a percentage cannot answer ("does the task I am about to start
still fit?"). The rate is an exponential moving average of the context growth
between renders, persisted per session; samples shorter than 15 seconds are
dropped as noise, and the negative jump a compaction produces is skipped rather
than fed in (the window shrank, the burn rate did not change). A long gap with
no growth decays the rate, so an idle session does not keep claiming an
hour-old pace. Colour: dim >= 30 min, plain 15\(en29, yellow 5\(en14, red < 5.
Beyond 10 hours nothing is rendered \(em that is not a number anyone acts on.
Needs at least two renders to have a rate at all.
.PP
.B response:
the API time the last completed turn took, as the delta of the cumulative
.I total_api_duration_ms
between two renders. It keeps showing that value until a new turn produces a
new one \(em a render without an API call in between (a plain refresh) would
otherwise blank it, and "the last response took 4.1s" stays true meanwhile.
This is the live counterpart to
.BR "claudii perf" ,
which has the aggregates: it is what separates "the model is thinking" from
"the API is slow today" \(em and the
.B claude-status
segment next to it says which.
.PP
.B branch:
the branch nothing else showed. The
.B worktree
segment only names a branch when the session actually runs inside a worktree,
so a plain checkout rendered no branch at all. Dim by default; yellow when
uncommitted changes are sitting directly on
.BR main / master / trunk
\(em WIP that wants a feature branch, a nudge rather than an alarm \(em and
yellow
.B \[u2387] detached
on a detached HEAD. It shares the single
.B git status
fork with
.B git-sync
(and
.BR ci ,
which needs the branch name to look up the right run), so a layout using all
three still scans the working tree once.
.PP
.B ci:
the newest GitHub Actions run on the branch this session is on \(em
.B CI \[u2713]
green success,
.B CI \[u2717]
red failure,
.B CI \[u25CF]
cyan in progress,
.B CI \[u25CC]
dim queued,
.B CI \[u2298]
dim cancelled/skipped/neutral, and a dim
.B CI ?
when the refresh itself broke (an indicator whose lookup failed must never be
indistinguishable from a green one). A branch with no runs yet, a repo with no
GitHub remote, and a missing
.BR gh (1)
render nothing at all \(em those are permanent states, and a permanent glyph
would be permanent noise. Past an hour without a successful refresh the segment
reads
.BR "CI stale" .
No render ever makes a network call: the segment reads a cache file that
.BR claudii-ci-refresh (1)
maintains in the background, keyed per repo
.I and
branch so parallel sessions and worktrees never fight over one entry. The TTL
(\fBci.cache_ttl\fR, default 300s) adapts \(em a run in flight is polled 10x
more often, a branch with no runs backs off 6x. Opt-in: not in the default
layout, since it needs
.BR gh (1)
and a GitHub-hosted repo.
.PP
.B worktrees:
.B \[u2442]N
counts the
.I linked
worktrees of the repo the session is working in \(em the main working tree is
never counted, so N is exactly what
.B git worktree remove
can act on. Leftover orchestration worktrees are what this exists for: from
inside the main checkout they are invisible, they keep branches and stale state
alive, and they accumulate across waves. Worktrees git reports as
.I prunable
(their directory is gone, only stale admin data is left) are called out
separately as a yellow
.BR \[u232B]P ,
since those are garbage rather than parallel work \(em clear them with
.BR "git worktree prune" .
The worktree this session itself runs in is named by the
.B worktree
segment instead. One
.B git worktree list
fork per render, only when the segment is in the active layout; empty at zero
linked worktrees and outside a git repo.
.PP
.B rate-5h / rate-7d:
color is cyan < 50%, yellow 50\(en79%, red >= 80%.
Set
.B statusline.rate_display
to
.B remaining
to flip percentages from "used" to "what's left"
(e.g.\&
.B 5h:62%
becomes
.BR 5h:38% ).
Color thresholds always key off the underlying usage.
.PP
.B clock:
when
.B cc-insomnii
is on
.B PATH
and
.B statusline.insomnii
is
.B auto
(default) or
.BR on ,
claudii pipes the upstream JSON to cc-insomnii and uses its output verbatim.
Set
.B statusline.insomnii
to
.B off
to suppress.
Bedtime forwarded as
.B CC_INSOMNII_BEDTIME
from
.B statusline.bedtime
(default
.IR 23:00 ).
.PP
.B cron / Stop-hook integration:
.B claudii-stop-hook
reads the Stop-hook JSON that Claude Code fires after every session turn and
extracts
.B session_crons
(array of scheduled tasks with a
.B next_run_at
timestamp) and
.B background_tasks
(sibling array).
The earliest
.B next_run_at
is persisted as
.B next_cron_at=<epoch>
in the per-session cache.
To enable, add
.B claudii-stop-hook
to the
.B hooks.Stop
array in
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json :
.PP
.RS
.EX
"hooks": {
  "Stop": [{ "type": "command", "command": "claudii-stop-hook" }]
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.B cron
segment then renders
.B \[u23F0] <relative>
(e.g.\&
.BR "\[u23F0] 42m" )
when the epoch is in the future.
Not in the default layout \(em add
.B cron
to
.B statusline.lines
to opt in.
.B claudii se
also shows the glyph inline per session row when cached.
.PP
.B Terminal notifications (terminalSequence):
with
.B notifications.enabled
set to
.IR true ,
the hooks emit
.B terminalSequence
JSON (Claude Code \(>= 2.1.141) so the terminal shows alerts without a
controlling tty (works in tmux):
.B claudii-stop-hook
sets the window title when a monitored model is
.I down
(\fBnotifications.model_down\fR) and rings the bell when the cached burn-ETA
drops below
.B notifications.burn_eta_threshold_min
minutes (\fBnotifications.burn_eta\fR);
.B claudii-session-end-hook
(wire into
.BR hooks.SessionEnd )
sends a desktop notification with the session cost on real session
termination.
.B notifications.osc
picks the notify flavor:
.I 777
(Ghostty, urxvt, Warp) or
.I 9
(iTerm2, WezTerm, Windows Terminal).
.PP
.RS
.EX
"hooks": {
  "Stop":       [{ "type": "command", "command": "claudii-stop-hook" }],
  "SessionEnd": [{ "type": "command", "command": "claudii-session-end-hook" }]
}
.EE
.RE
.
